var GUIDES = [
 {cat:'Seasonal',color:'#1a6b8a',title:'Best Time to Visit Each Country',min:3,desc:'When to go for warm water, fewer crowds and better prices.',
  html:'<p>Mediterranean beaches are at their best from late May to mid-June and again in September: warm sea, long days and noticeably fewer people than in July and August. Sea temperature lags the air, so water is usually warmest in late summer, while the first half of June can still feel fresh to swim in. Every beach page on EuroShores lists a typical in-season water temperature and a recommended window, and this guide pulls those together by country.</p><h3>Country by country</h3><ul><li><b>Greece:</b> May&#8211;October on the islands, with the warmest sea in August and September. Beaches such as <b>Elafonissi Beach</b>, <b>Balos Lagoon</b> and <b>Porto Katsiki</b> are best from late May to June or in September, when the boats and car parks are calmer. The Aegean meltemi wind is strongest in July and August and can make exposed beaches choppy, so check the forecast for the side of the island you are on.</li><li><b>Italy:</b> June&#8211;September for swimming, May and October for walking and quiet. Sardinia\'s famous coves such as <b>Cala Mariolu</b> and <b>La Pelosa</b> have visitor limits or booking rules in peak season, so plan ahead for July and August. Amalfi-coast style beaches like <b>Spiaggia Grande</b> in Positano are far pleasanter in May, June and September.</li><li><b>Spain:</b> May&#8211;October on the Balearic Islands, where <b>Es Trenc</b>, <b>Ses Illetes</b> and <b>Cala Macarelleta</b> are lovely in June and September. The Atlantic north is different: <b>As Catedrais</b> in Galicia is best from May to September, and the tide matters more than the month.</li><li><b>Portugal (Algarve):</b> June&#8211;September for the beach, with May and October often sunny too. The Atlantic stays cooler than the Mediterranean all year, so water that feels bracing in June is milder by September. <b>Praia da Marinha</b>, <b>Benagil Cove</b> and <b>Praia Dona Ana</b> are best on calm-sea days.</li><li><b>Croatia:</b> June&#8211;September for swimming. <b>Zlatni Rat</b>, <b>Punta Rata</b> and <b>Banje Beach</b> are busy in July and August, so go early or choose a shoulder month.</li><li><b>Montenegro:</b> June&#8211;September. <b>Sveti Stefan</b> and <b>Jaz Beach</b> are at their liveliest in July and August, with festivals and concerts at Jaz.</li><li><b>Albania:</b> June&#8211;September. The Riviera at <b>Dhermi</b>, <b>Jale</b> and <b>Ksamil Beaches</b> fills in August, while June and September bring warm water and easier parking.</li><li><b>Malta and Gozo:</b> June&#8211;September for the beach, with May and October still good for <b>Blue Lagoon</b> and <b>St Peter\'s Pool</b>. Sandy bays such as <b>Golden Bay</b> and <b>Ramla Bay</b> are crowded on summer weekends.</li><li><b>Corsica (France):</b> June&#8211;September. <b>Palombaggia</b>, <b>Santa Giulia</b> and <b>Saleccia</b> are best in June or September, when the sea is warm and the roads are quieter.</li></ul><h3>Why shoulder season works</h3><p>In July and August the most famous beaches fill by late morning, parking is the first thing to go, and boat-only or booking-limited beaches can sell out days ahead. Late May, June and September give you most of the warmth with far more space, and accommodation is usually cheaper. The trade-off is that some beach bars, boat trips and shuttle services run reduced timetables in the first and last weeks of the season.</p><h3>Picking the right month for your priorities</h3><ul><li><b>Warmest water:</b> late August to mid-September in most of the Mediterranean.</li><li><b>Fewest people:</b> the last week of May, the first half of June and the second half of September.</li><li><b>Best light for photos:</b> early morning in June or September, before crowds and haze build up.</li><li><b>Lowest prices:</b> the shoulder months, and midweek stays rather than weekends.</li></ul><h3>Practical tips</h3><p>Check live conditions on the day: wind, swell, jellyfish alerts and any access restrictions. Some beaches have temporary closures or booking systems that change from year to year, and each beach page notes when its information was last checked. If your dates are fixed in July or August, pick the most remote beaches on our list and arrive before 9 AM.</p>'},
 {cat:'Budget',color:'#e8a020',title:'Budget Beach Holidays in Europe',min:2,desc:'Enjoy great shores without overspending.',
  html:'<p>Beaches themselves are free almost everywhere in Europe; costs come from sunbeds, parking, boat access and food. With a little planning you can enjoy some of the continent\'s best shores on a modest budget. This guide covers where the money really goes and how to keep it in your pocket.</p><h3>Where the money goes</h3><ul><li><b>Sunbed and umbrella hire:</b> often the biggest daily cost at developed beaches such as <b>Spiaggia Grande</b> or <b>Ghadira Bay</b>, and priced highest in July and August.</li><li><b>Parking:</b> paid, and sometimes limited, at beaches such as <b>Zlatni Rat</b> and <b>Palombaggia</b>.</li><li><b>Boats and tickets:</b> some beaches need a boat or a booking, for example <b>Blue Lagoon</b> and <b>Balos Lagoon</b>.</li><li><b>Food and drink:</b> beach bars charge more than village shops, and prices drop noticeably a few streets inland.</li></ul><h3>Cheap and free beaches worth the trip</h3><ul><li><b>Es Trenc</b> in Mallorca has long stretches with no sunbeds, so you can bring your own towel and shade.</li><li><b>Banje Beach</b> in Dubrovnik is free to enter, with a paid beach club alongside.</li><li><b>Bacvice</b> in Split is a city beach reachable on foot, so you skip car costs entirely.</li><li><b>Ksamil Beaches</b>, <b>Dhermi</b> and <b>Drymades</b> in Albania are among Europe\'s lower-cost coastal destinations, with free areas next to the paid beach clubs.</li><li><b>Praia da Marinha</b> and <b>Praia Dona Ana</b> in the Algarve need only a car or a short walk.</li></ul><h3>Money-saving habits</h3><ul><li>Travel in shoulder season: late May, June and September have lower flights and accommodation than July and August.</li><li>Bring your own umbrella and snacks instead of renting sunbeds and buying lunch on the sand.</li><li>Stay in the next village rather than right at the beach, then walk, cycle or take public transport in.</li><li>Choose self-catering, so a supermarket shop replaces some restaurant meals.</li><li>Carry cash. Some small beach kiosks, parking attendants and boat operators do not take cards.</li><li>Arrive early. Free parking spots go first and early arrivals often avoid sunbed fees altogether.</li></ul><h3>Where to save, and where not to</h3><p>Do not skimp on safety. Avoid unofficial boat operators without life jackets, and do not leave valuables unattended. Spend a little more on sturdy water shoes if a beach is pebbly or rocky, such as <b>Stiniva</b> or <b>Gjipe</b>. It is better to pay for a licensed boat to a remote beach than to risk an unsafe path.</p><h3>Sample budget idea</h3><p>A week split between a village base and day trips by bus, with a picnic on most days and one beach-club day as a treat, costs far less than a week of resort sunbeds. Prices change from season to season and by country, so check current rates before you book.</p>'},
 {cat:'Family',color:'#2ecc71',title:'Best Family-Friendly Beaches',min:2,desc:'Shallow water, easy access and facilities.',
  html:'<p>The best beaches for children combine gentle entry, shallow water, easy access and some facilities. This guide shortlists the easiest options on our list and explains what to check before you go. Every beach page includes an access rating and a note on facilities, so you can compare quickly.</p><h3>What makes a beach family friendly</h3><ul><li><b>Shallow, gradual entry:</b> toddlers and weaker swimmers can paddle safely.</li><li><b>Easy access:</b> a short flat walk, with no long staircase or steep path that is hard with a pushchair.</li><li><b>Facilities:</b> toilets, showers, shade and somewhere to buy water or food.</li><li><b>Sand rather than sharp pebbles:</b> though water shoes help on pebble beaches.</li><li><b>Lifeguards:</b> especially in July and August, though coverage varies by beach and by season.</li></ul><h3>Our top picks for families</h3><ul><li><b>Rajska Plaza</b> on Rab: a long sandy beach where the shallow water reaches far out, with bars, showers and lifeguards.</li><li><b>Ramla Bay</b> and <b>Ghadira Bay</b> in Malta: wide sandy bays with gentle entry and nearby facilities.</li><li><b>Es Trenc</b> in Mallorca: soft sand and shallow water, with a few simple beach bars.</li><li><b>Ses Illetes</b> in Formentera: very shallow turquoise water, although it is busy in summer.</li><li><b>La Pelosa</b> in Sardinia: shallow, calm water. Check the current access rules, because visitor numbers are managed in peak season.</li><li><b>Palombaggia</b> and <b>Santa Giulia</b> in Corsica: fine sand and calm bays with facilities nearby.</li><li><b>Bacvice</b> in Split: a city beach with shallow, sandy-bottomed water and plenty of nearby cafes.</li><li><b>Golden Bay</b> in Malta: a wide sandy bay with facilities and a lifeguard in season.</li></ul><h3>Beaches to skip with very young children</h3><p>Some of the most photogenic beaches are poor choices for small children. <b>Cala Goloritze</b>, <b>Stiniva</b> and <b>Gjipe</b> require a challenging walk or a boat. <b>Navagio Beach</b> is currently closed to landings. <b>Praia da Marinha</b> has steep access and rocky ground. Check each beach page for its access rating before you plan.</p><h3>Safety checklist</h3><ul><li>Check wind and wave conditions on the day, and look for flag systems where they exist.</li><li>Use sun protection and shade, and bring more water than you think you need.</li><li>Keep children within arm\'s reach near the water, even in shallow bays.</li><li>Bring water shoes for pebble beaches and rocky entries.</li><li>Arrive early in July and August, because parking and shade fill up fast.</li></ul><h3>Best months with children</h3><p>June and September are often ideal: warm enough to swim, with fewer crowds and lower prices. In the peak weeks of July and August, plan around the heat by going to the beach early morning or late afternoon.</p>'},
 {cat:'Active',color:'#9b59b6',title:'Top Beaches for Water Sports',min:2,desc:'Wind, waves and clear water.',
  html:'<p>Europe\'s beaches suit very different water sports, from windsurfing on breezy headlands to kayaking in sheltered coves. This guide groups our beaches by activity and explains what to check first: wind direction, equipment hire and local rules. Conditions change with the season and the day, so treat the list as a starting point rather than a promise.</p><h3>Windsurfing and kitesurfing</h3><ul><li><b>Zlatni Rat</b> on Brac is widely known for windsurfing, because the afternoon wind builds along the spit; hire and lessons are available in season.</li><li><b>Ghadira Bay</b> in Malta has a long, shallow bay with a range of water-sports operators in summer.</li><li><b>Golden Bay</b> in Malta can offer wind and waves on exposed days, so check conditions before you go.</li><li><b>Es Trenc</b> in Mallorca is a long open beach where wind sports are possible on breezy days.</li></ul><h3>Kayaking and paddleboarding</h3><ul><li><b>Ksamil Beaches</b> in Albania: calm water around small islets makes paddling easy in summer.</li><li><b>Banje Beach</b> in Dubrovnik: sea kayaks are a popular way to see the walls and nearby islands from the water.</li><li><b>Sakarun Beach</b> on Dugi Otok: sheltered, shallow water is good for paddleboards on calm days.</li><li><b>Ses Illetes</b> in Formentera: very shallow, clear water suits paddleboarding on windless mornings.</li><li><b>Cala Goloritze</b> and <b>Cala Mariolu</b> in Sardinia: reached by boat or kayak from the coast in season, but follow local rules and sea conditions.</li></ul><h3>Boat trips and cave exploring</h3><ul><li><b>Benagil Cove</b> in the Algarve: boat and kayak tours go into the sea cave, which is a rocky coast that needs calm seas. Check current rules, because access to the cave beach is regulated.</li><li><b>Blue Lagoon</b> at Comino: small boats shuttle visitors from Malta and Gozo in summer.</li><li><b>Balos Lagoon</b> in Crete: reached by boat or by a long track from the car park.</li><li><b>Stiniva</b> on Vis: best reached by boat or kayak, because the cove is entered through a narrow gap.</li></ul><h3>Safety and etiquette</h3><ul><li>Check the wind, swell and local flag system before you launch. Offshore winds can push you away from the coast.</li><li>Wear a life jacket on boards and kayaks, and tell someone where you are going.</li><li>Stay out of swimming areas that are marked with buoys, and keep clear of boat lanes.</li><li>Use licensed operators for rentals and boat trips, and ask about insurance and safety kit.</li><li>Do not drop anchor on seagrass meadows, which are protected in many Mediterranean areas.</li></ul><h3>When to go</h3><p>Winds are often steadiest in July and August in the Aegean and Adriatic, while June and September give calmer seas and lighter crowds. Atlantic beaches like <b>Praia da Marinha</b> can have strong swell outside summer, so beginners should stick to calm days.</p>'},
 {cat:'Romance',color:'#e74c3c',title:'Most Romantic Beach Getaways',min:2,desc:'Secluded coves and sunset views.',
  html:'<p>A romantic beach is as much about atmosphere as sand: a dramatic setting, privacy, sunset light and somewhere to sit with a drink afterwards. These beaches on our list stand out for couples, whether you want a quiet cove or a lively evening promenade. Check each beach page for access, season and current rules before you go.</p><h3>Quiet coves and hidden spots</h3><ul><li><b>Cala Goloritze</b> in Sardinia: a pebble cove under a limestone needle, reached on foot with a challenging walk. Access is regulated, so check the current rules.</li><li><b>Cala Mariolu</b> in Sardinia: pale pebbles and clear water. It is usually visited by boat or by a long walk from the coast.</li><li><b>Stiniva</b> on Vis: a pebble cove that is entered through a narrow rock gap and feels hidden from the sea.</li><li><b>Dubovica</b> on Hvar: a small pebble cove down a steep path, ideal if you start early and bring supplies.</li><li><b>San Blas Bay</b> on Gozo: a secluded cove down a steep road, rewarding for those who plan their return climb.</li><li><b>Gjipe</b> in Albania: a canyon-framed beach reached by a walk, with a wild feel.</li></ul><h3>Sunset and evening views</h3><ul><li><b>Elafonissi Beach</b> in Crete: pink-tinted sand and shallow lagoons, lovely in late afternoon after day visitors leave.</li><li><b>Potami Beach</b> near Himara: a long pebble curve that is a good place for a sunset swim.</li><li><b>Ses Illetes</b> in Formentera: shallow turquoise water and sunset views over the Mediterranean.</li><li><b>Sveti Stefan</b> in Montenegro: a famous island village view, best at golden hour from the beach.</li></ul><h3>Dramatic scenery</h3><ul><li><b>Praia da Marinha</b> in the Algarve: cliffs, arches and clear water, best on a calm day.</li><li><b>Benagil Cove</b> in the Algarve: a sea cave with an opening to the sky.</li><li><b>As Catedrais</b> in Galicia: arches and caves that you can walk through at low tide, so check the tide table.</li><li><b>Qeparo</b> in Albania: a curved beach with clear water and an old village above.</li></ul><h3>Planning a romantic day</h3><ul><li>Go early or late, because the middle of the day is when day trippers arrive.</li><li>Pack food, water and shade for remote coves, where there are often no facilities.</li><li>Check the tide, wind and any booking rules in advance.</li><li>Wear proper shoes for the walk, and keep valuables light.</li></ul><h3>A note on <b>Navagio Beach</b></h3><p>Navagio on Zakynthos is one of Europe\'s best-known beaches, but landings are currently banned because of landslide risk. You can still see the cove from the cliff viewing platform, if it is open. Confirm the current situation before you plan a trip.</p>'},
 {cat:'Diving',color:'#1abc9c',title:'Best Snorkelling &amp; Diving Spots',min:2,desc:'Clear water and life beneath the surface.',
  html:'<p>Clear water, rocky shelves and sheltered coves make some beaches excellent for snorkelling and shore diving. This guide lists the spots on our list where visibility is typically good, along with what to check before you get in the water. Marine life varies by season and by site, and conditions can change from day to day.</p><h3>Best snorkelling spots on our list</h3><ul><li><b>Blue Lagoon</b> at Comino: shallow, clear water over pale sand and rock; very busy in peak season, so go early.</li><li><b>St Peter\'s Pool</b> in Malta: a natural rock pool with clear water, popular with swimmers and snorkellers. It has no facilities, so bring water and shoes.</li><li><b>Ksamil Beaches</b> in Albania: small islets and clear shallows close to shore.</li><li><b>Stiniva</b> on Vis: a sheltered cove with clear water beneath steep cliffs.</li><li><b>Cala Mariolu</b> and <b>Cala Goloritze</b> in Sardinia: very clear water along rocky shores, usually reached by boat or by a long walk.</li><li><b>Saleccia</b> in Corsica: a wide bay with clear turquoise water.</li><li><b>Sarakiniko</b> on Milos: white volcanic rock and clear blue water, good for swimming and snorkelling from the rock ledges.</li><li><b>Potami Beach</b> near Himara: clear water fed by freshwater springs.</li></ul><h3>Sheltered bays for beginners</h3><ul><li><b>Ses Illetes</b> in Formentera: shallow and calm on windless days.</li><li><b>Cala Macarelleta</b> in Menorca: a small, sheltered cove with clear water.</li><li><b>Ghajn Tuffieha Bay</b> and <b>Ramla Bay</b> in Malta: sandy bays where the water is clear in calm weather, although waves can be rough in strong wind.</li></ul><h3>Atlantic coves</h3><p><b>Praia da Marinha</b> and <b>Praia Dona Ana</b> in the Algarve have rock formations and clear water on calm days, but the Atlantic is cooler and swell can arrive quickly. A thicker wetsuit top helps in June and September.</p><h3>What to bring</h3><ul><li>Mask, snorkel and fins, or hire them locally in season.</li><li>A rash vest or thin wetsuit for sun and for cooler water.</li><li>Water shoes for rocky entries and sea urchins.</li><li>A surface marker buoy if you swim away from the shore.</li><li>Reef-safe sun protection and plenty of drinking water.</li></ul><h3>Safety and the sea</h3><ul><li>Check wind, swell and currents before you go in, and never snorkel alone.</li><li>Stay away from boat lanes, and keep to marked swimming areas where they exist.</li><li>Watch for sea urchins and jellyfish, and do not touch marine life.</li><li>Do not take shells, corals or other natural items, and avoid trampling seagrass.</li><li>Scuba diving needs a certified operator, so ask local dive centres about courses, guided dives and rules.</li></ul><h3>When to go</h3><p>Water clarity is usually good in late spring and early summer, while sea temperature peaks in late summer. Weekday mornings are best for calm water and fewer people in the bay.</p>'}
];

